---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import AssistantSetup from "../components/AssistantSetup.astro";
import { STANDING_LINE } from "@milton/theme/facts";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Connect your AI | Milton"
  description="Connect your AI and start with about five things you wrote and sent, found by your AI in your email or pasted. Setup guidance and connection requirements for Milton."
  legal
>
  <p class="eyebrow">## A little paperwork. Once.</p>
  <h1>Connect your AI.</h1>
  <p>
    <strong>Connect Milton. Say “set up Milton.”</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    If your AI can already search your email, it offers to find about five
    emails you wrote and sent and shows you each one in full. Milton keeps only
    the ones you approve, exactly as shown, so drafts start from how you
    actually write. Otherwise it asks you to paste a few. Milton never reads
    your inbox.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then it shows you the standing line. Have something to fix right now? Paste
    it and ask your AI to “clean this up.” It should fix what you asked, keep
    the rest in your words, and briefly list the changes. Setup can wait.
  </p>
  <p>
    Milton connects through MCP, a standard that lets AIs use tools. You need a
    client that supports remote MCP, OAuth sign-in, and tools that read and
    save. Your plan or workspace administrator may restrict these features.
  </p>
  <p>
    First, <a href="https://app.meetmilton.com/login">sign in to Milton</a> with
    Google or GitHub. Use the same account when your AI asks you to authorize
    the connection. The first 30 days are free; no credit card is required.
  </p>
  <div class="guided-setup">
    <AssistantSetup />
  </div>
  <div class="connection sheet">
    <div class="sheet-bar">Milton’s MCP server URL</div>
    <div class="sheet-content">
      <code>https://mcp.meetmilton.com/mcp</code>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div id="standing-line" class="standing">
    <p>
      Every setup includes a line for your AI’s standing instructions, asking it
      to read Milton before writing:
    </p>
    <blockquote>{STANDING_LINE}</blockquote>
    <p>
      Your AI shows it and adds it once you approve, or you paste it yourself;
      each AI below says where it goes. This is a reminder, not a guarantee. Ask
      “Did you get that memo?” and verify a successful read.
    </p>
  </div>
  <nav class="clients" aria-label="Choose your AI">
    <a href="#claude">Claude</a>
    <a href="#chatgpt">ChatGPT</a>
    <a href="#claude-code">Claude Code</a>
    <a href="#codex">Codex</a>
    <a href="#cursor">Cursor</a>
  </nav>
  <section id="claude" aria-labelledby="claude-heading">
    <h2 id="claude-heading">Claude</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Open Claude’s connector settings and add a custom connector named
        Milton.
      </li>
      <li>
        Use the server URL above, then complete the sign-in and authorization
        prompts.
      </li>
      <li>
        Enable Milton in the conversation’s connectors menu before asking Claude
        to use it.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Claude supports custom connectors across its plans; Free accounts have a
      one-custom-connector limit. Workspaces may need an owner or administrator
      to add it.{" "}
      <a href="https://support.claude.com/en/articles/11175166-get-started-with-custom-connectors-using-remote-mcp">
        Claude’s current connector guide ↗
      </a>
    </p>
    <p>
      As part of setup, add <a href="#standing-line">the standing line</a> to
      your profile preferences, a project’s instructions, or a memory.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="chatgpt" aria-labelledby="chatgpt-heading">
    <h2 id="chatgpt-heading">ChatGPT</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Open Plugins and look for the option to add a custom MCP server.</li>
      <li>
        Add Milton with the server URL above and complete the sign-in prompts.
      </li>
      <li>
        Make Milton available in your conversation before asking ChatGPT to read
        your memos or keep one.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Custom MCP access depends on your plan and workspace settings. You need
      both read and write tools; a search-only connection won’t keep memos. If
      the option is missing, check the{" "}
      <a href="https://developers.openai.com/plugins/build/app-quickstart">
        official connection guide ↗
      </a>{" "}
      before signing up for a different plan.
    </p>
    <p>
      As part of setup, add <a href="#standing-line">the standing line</a> to
      your custom instructions or a memory.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="claude-code" aria-labelledby="claude-code-heading">
    <h2 id="claude-code-heading">Claude Code</h2>
    <p>In your terminal, add Milton for your user account:</p>
    <pre><code>claude mcp add --transport http --scope user milton https://mcp.meetmilton.com/mcp</code></pre>
    <p>
      Then open Claude Code, run <code>/mcp</code>, select Milton, and complete
      authentication. User scope makes the connection available across projects.{" "}
      <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp">
        Claude Code’s MCP guide ↗
      </a>
    </p>
    <p>
      Setup offers to add <a href="#standing-line">the standing line</a> to
      CLAUDE.md in your home directory (<code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>), so it
      covers every project. Claude Code adds it only once you approve; if it
      doesn’t, paste it there yourself.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="codex" aria-labelledby="codex-heading">
    <h2 id="codex-heading">Codex</h2>
    <p>With the Codex CLI installed, run:</p>
    <pre><code>{`codex mcp add milton --url https://mcp.meetmilton.com/mcp
codex mcp login milton`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Complete the browser authorization. If adding Milton already signed you
      in, you don’t need to sign in again. Start a new Codex session if the
      tools aren’t available in the current one.{" "}
      <a href="https://developers.openai.com/codex/mcp">Codex’s MCP guide ↗</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      Setup offers to add <a href="#standing-line">the standing line</a> to
      AGENTS.md in your Codex home directory (<code>~/.codex/AGENTS.md</code>),
      so it covers every project. Codex adds it only once you approve; if it
      doesn’t, paste it there yourself. Without it, Codex often doesn’t read
      Milton.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="cursor" aria-labelledby="cursor-heading">
    <h2 id="cursor-heading">Cursor</h2>
    <p>
      Add a remote MCP server named Milton in Cursor’s MCP configuration, using
      the server URL above. Cursor supports HTTP connections and OAuth sign-in.
      Complete authentication and enable the tools for your agent.{" "}
      <a href="https://cursor.com/docs/mcp">
        Cursor’s setup and configuration guide ↗
      </a>
    </p>
    <p>
      As part of setup, paste <a href="#standing-line">the standing line</a>
      into User Rules in Cursor’s settings, so it covers every project.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="first-preference" aria-labelledby="first-heading">
    <h2 id="first-heading">Start with your own words.</h2>
    <p>
      New accounts start empty. If your AI already has your email connected, it
      can offer to find about five emails you sent in the last 90 days. It reads
      at most about 30, shows you each candidate in full, and keeps only the
      ones you approve by number, exactly as shown. Your AI uses its own
      connection; Milton never reads your inbox and gets no email permissions.
      The option appears only when your AI can already search your email; it
      won’t ask you to connect it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Otherwise, paste about five emails, messages or posts you wrote and sent,
      unedited. Your AI keeps each as a memo, exactly as you pasted it. Either
      way it should use only your own writing, skipping automated, forwarded and
      other people’s text. No emails to start? Just write. Each final you accept
      becomes a memo, and drafts sound like you within a handful of them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Or start with a writing task: paste an email or DM to clean up, or ask for
      a new draft. Your AI reads what you have on file and should change only
      what you asked. You choose the final text.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you accept a final, by approving it, saying it’s done, or copying it
      to send, your AI keeps it as a memo without asking: its first draft, your
      final and a one-line summary of the situation. Milton’s code works out
      your tweaks, the changes you made to the draft. Your AI tells you in one
      line, such as “Kept memo: vendor decline.” Whether that line shows depends
      on your AI app. Next time, any connected AI gets the finals that match,
      with what you changed in them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Milton keeps no standing rules. When you say “remember …” (“no em
      dashes”), your AI applies it and keeps it in its own memory or
      instructions; the changes you make to its drafts are kept as tweaks
      anyway. <a href="https://app.meetmilton.com/">Open Milton</a> to see your
      memos and their tweaks, forget a memo or turn off Keep memos.
    </p>
    <p>In your next conversation, ask:</p>
    <blockquote>“Did you get that memo?”</blockquote>
    <p>“Did you get the memo?” also works.</p>
    <p>
      Use this to ask your AI to read your memos again, even if it read them
      earlier. It should confirm only after a successful read. This requests a
      read, not a save.
    </p>
    <p>
      The standing instruction asks your AI to read its report before writing.
      Check that it actually does. Every connected AI can read the finals in its
      reports, so keep private project context in its own tools.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="help-heading">
    <h2 id="help-heading">Not connecting?</h2>
    <p>
      Check the server URL, sign in with the same Milton account, and confirm
      your AI allows the connector in this conversation. Workspace policies may
      require administrator approval. For help, email{" "}
      <a href="mailto:support@meetmilton.com">support@meetmilton.com</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/pricing">See pricing →</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>

<style>
  /* No paragraph ends on a lone word. */
  p,
  blockquote {
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .guided-setup {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
    width: fit-content;
  }
  h1 {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }
  section {
    scroll-margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }
  .connection,
  .standing {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }
  .standing {
    scroll-margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }
  .clients {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-2) var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }
  .clients a {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: var(--touch-target);
  }
  ol {
    padding-left: var(--space-6);
  }
  li + li {
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
  }
  pre {
    white-space: pre-wrap;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    background: var(--surface);
    padding: var(--space-5);
    border: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    border-radius: var(--radius-control);
    font-size: var(--text-small);
  }
  blockquote {
    margin: var(--space-5) 0;
    padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-5);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--sheet-rule);
    background: var(--surface);
  }
</style>
